    "title": "Unbounded strong-product ratios for the Grundy domination number",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23607502",
    "publication_date": "2026-10-24",
    "description": "<p>We prove that strong-product ratios for the Grundy domination number are unbounded. For each m &gt;= 5, graphs on the five-element subsets of [m] have Grundy domination numbers at most m and binom(m,2), while their strong product has an independent set of size binom(m,5). The resulting lower bound is (m-2)(m-3)(m-4)/(60m). The proof uses exact closed-neighbourhood support matrices over F_2 and F_3, following Alon's classical intersection-congruence method. We also give an elementary half-graph proof that gamma_gr(H_N)=floor((N+1)/3)+3 for every N &gt;= 6. Its product with the octahedron gives a 114-vertex counterexample. Both general arguments are written proofs independent of computation. The separate Lean 4 certificate covers only the original N=21 example. This revision is prepared for submission to Annals of Mathematics; it is not an accepted journal article. AI assistance and the affected proof sections are disclosed in the manuscript.</p>",
